XV6 System Calls and Scheduling Implementation
This project extends the XV6 operating system by implementing new system calls and scheduling policies as specified. Below is an overview of the implemented features, instructions for compilation, and details for testing.
Features Implemented
System Calls

getSysCount and syscount:

Description: The getSysCount system call counts the number of times a specified system call is invoked by a process and its children. The syscount user program takes a mask (e.g., 1 << i for the i-th syscall) and a command to execute, then prints the count in the format: PID <pid> called <syscall name> <n> times.
Files Modified:
kernel/syscall.h: Added syscall number and name mapping.
kernel/syscall.c: Implemented getSysCount and tracking mechanism.
user/syscount.c: User program to parse mask and execute command.
kernel/proc.h, kernel/proc.c: Added syscall count tracking in struct proc.




sigalarm and sigreturn:

Description: The sigalarm(interval, handler) system call triggers a handler function every interval CPU ticks for the calling process. sigreturn restores the process state after the handler executes.
Files Modified:
kernel/syscall.h: Added syscall numbers.
kernel/syscall.c, kernel/sysproc.c: Implemented sigalarm and sigreturn.
kernel/proc.h, kernel/proc.c: Added fields for alarm interval and handler.
kernel/trap.c: Modified to invoke,新2.0 invoke handler on CPU ticks.





Scheduling Policies
The default round-robin scheduler is extended with two new policies, selectable at compile time via the SCHEDULER macro.

Lottery Based Scheduling (LBS):

Description: A preemptive scheduler where processes are assigned tickets (default: 1). The probability of a process running is proportional to its ticket count. If multiple processes have the same ticket count, the earliest-arrived process wins.
System Call: settickets(int number) sets the number of tickets for the calling process.
Files Modified:
kernel/proc.h, kernel/proc.c: Added ticket count and arrival time to struct proc.
kernel/syscall.h, kernel/sysproc.c: Implemented settickets.
kernel/scheduler.c: Modified to implement lottery scheduling.




Multi-Level Feedback Queue (MLFQ):

Description: A preemptive scheduler with four priority queues (0 highest, 3 lowest). Processes start in queue 0 and move to lower queues if they consume their full time slice (1, 4, 8, 16 ticks for queues 0-3). I/O-bound processes return to their previous queue. Priority boosting moves all processes to queue 0 every 48 ticks.
Files Modified:
kernel/proc.h, kernel/proc.c: Added queue ID and time slice tracking.
kernel/scheduler.c: Implemented MLFQ logic.
kernel/trap.c: Adjusted for time slice enforcement.





Compilation Instructions
To compile XV6 with a specific scheduling policy, use the following commands:
make clean
make qemu SCHEDULER=<POLICY>

Replace <POLICY> with:

RR for Round-Robin (default)
LBS for Lottery Based Scheduling
MLFQ for Multi-Level Feedback Queue

Example:
make clean
make qemu SCHEDULER=MLFQ

Testing

System Calls:

Test syscount:syscount 32768 grep hello README.md

Expected output: PID <pid> called open <n> times
Test sigalarm and sigreturn with a user program that sets an alarm and handler, e.g., in user/alarmtest.c.


Scheduling:

Use procdump (Ctrl-P) to inspect process states and scheduling behavior.
Run schedulertest to measure average waiting and running times for performance comparison.
For MLFQ, generate a timeline graph using schedulertest with varying CPU burst lengths. Plot queue ID vs. time with color-coded processes, ensuring priority boosts are visible.



Report
The report includes:

Brief explanation of implementation changes for each specification.
Performance comparison (average waiting/running times) for RR, LBS, and MLFQ using schedulertest on 1 CPU.
Analysis of LBS arrival time implication:
Implication: Prioritizing earlier-arrived processes with equal tickets ensures fairness for processes that joined earlier, preventing latecomers from dominating.
Pitfalls: If many processes arrive simultaneously with equal tickets, it may resemble FIFO, reducing randomness.
Equal Tickets Case: If all processes have the same tickets, the earliest-arrived process always wins, effectively becoming a FIFO scheduler.


MLFQ timeline graph showing queue transitions and priority boosts.

Notes

Ensure procdump is enhanced to display scheduling-specific details (e.g., ticket count for LBS, queue ID for MLFQ).
All processes in LBS must be in the RUNNABLE state to participate in the lottery.
MLFQ assumes preemption occurs at the end of each tick.
For LaTeX-based reports or graphs, use PDFLaTeX with packages from texlive-full and texlive-fonts-extra.

